WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:04.600
c. 650 · Monastic burial ground on
Llandough hill, above the Ely

2
00:00:12.000 --> 00:00:19.868
5th–11th century: an early-medieval
monastic cemetery grows on the hill,

3
00:00:19.908 --> 00:00:25.360
beside the site of an older Roman building
(PX-03)

4
00:00:33.050 --> 00:00:34.186
Earth to earth.

5
00:00:34.226 --> 00:00:39.279
Lie here with the others, your face to the
east, toward the dawn.

6
00:00:40.130 --> 00:00:46.081
There are so many of them, brother. Row
after row. Every winter, more.

7
00:00:46.121 --> 00:00:48.136
Will she be lonely here?

8
00:00:48.410 --> 00:00:53.951
Lonely? Never. The brothers sing the
psalms here every dusk, above the Ely.

9
00:00:53.991 --> 00:00:56.853
This little church was old when I came,

10
00:00:56.893 --> 00:00:59.681
and it will be here after we are gone.

11
00:01:05.250 --> 00:01:10.199
And under the turf, the old stones. The
spades still strike them.

12
00:01:11.210 --> 00:01:16.641
They are the Romans' stones. They are long
gone; we are the ones who stay.

13
00:01:16.681 --> 00:01:19.451
Come. The brothers are waiting for us.

14
00:01:21.200 --> 00:01:28.150
St Dochdwy's church, beside the later
farm, stands

15
00:01:28.190 --> 00:01:35.560
where archaeology places an early
monastery (CH-0001)
